  "response": "## Triage rule I applied\n\nNothing in this report is `high`. The distribution of `scores.agent_risk` is cleanly bimodal, and that — not severity — is what separates the actionable from the noise:\n\n| Band | `agent_risk` | Detectors in it | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Semantic | 0.28 – 0.45 | sync I/O, magic literal, dead code, role checks, dup blocks, dates | a future agent could get this *wrong* |\n| Cosmetic | 0.04 – 0.07 | deep import, design tokens, rasters | costs bytes or taste, not correctness |\n\nSo:\n\n- **Block merge** — `severity: high`, or `medium` + `agent_risk ≥ 0.50`. **Nothing in this report qualifies.** Max severity is `medium` (0.55), max agent_risk is 0.45.\n- **Warn** — `agent_risk ≥ 0.35` (the finding can actively mislead the next editor), *or* a cosmetic finding with a real user-facing cost.\n- **Ignore** — `agent_risk ≤ 0.10`, *or* the finding is an artifact of how this repo is laid out.\n\nThat last clause carries most of the weight. `coverage.by_package` shows 14 packages that are **tutorial snapshots of the same app**: `dashboard/final-example` (46 files) beside `dashboard/starter-example` (31), `seo` beside `seo/demo`, and six `basics/*` step directories. Every cross-file finding in this report pairs a file with its own sibling copy. That single fact reclassifies 46 of 99 findings.\n\n**Verdict: 0 block · 20 warn · 79 ignore.**\n\n---\n\n## Block merge\n\n**None.** State it plainly rather than manufacturing one: with zero `high` findings and a ceiling of `agent_risk` 0.45, no single-file crime here can silently change behaviour. What *would* flip a finding into this bucket:\n\n- any `duplicated_role_status_plan_check` where the differing expression shapes live in the **same** deployable app (here they don't — they're the final/starter pair), or\n- any `sync_io_in_hotpath` whose call site is a request path rather than build-time data fetching.\n\n---\n\n## Warn (20)\n\n### `commented_out_code` — 4 of 10 warn\n`severity: medium (0.45)` · `agent_risk: 0.39` · `confidence: 0.90`\n\n| id | file | size |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00007` | `starter-example/app/query/route.ts:21-25` | 5 lines, `try/catch/await/return` |\n| `crime_00008` | `starter-example/app/ui/dashboard/latest-invoices.tsx:19-56` | 38 lines |\n| `crime_00009` | `starter-example/app/ui/dashboard/revenue-chart.tsx:33-62` | 30 lines |\n| `crime_00010` | `starter-example/app/ui/invoices/pagination.tsx:17-50` | 34 lines |\n\nThese four are 30-ish-line disabled *implementations*, not commented notes. `agent_risk` 0.39 at `confidence` 0.90 is the detector saying the block is real code and it will read as a live candidate: an agent told to \"fix the pagination\" opens `pagination.tsx` and finds two implementations, one of which is invisible to the type checker. Effort is `quick`, fix is `delete`. Cheap enough that a warn should get resolved in this PR.\n\nCaveat worth writing in the PR comment: all four are in `starter-example`, where commented-out code is plausibly the *point* — the learner uncomments it. If that's confirmed, they drop to ignore and `dashboard/starter-example` belongs in the config exclude list, not in review.\n\n### `magic_domain_literal_scatter` — 1 warn\n`crime_00006` · `severity: medium (0.50)` · `agent_risk: 0.41` · `confidence: 0.85`\n\nThe literal `\"status\"` in 5 production files spanning `app/lib/actions.ts:43` (the server action) and both invoice forms. This is the second-highest `agent_risk` in the report and the score is doing real work that the other axes miss: `blast_radius` is **0** because the coupling isn't an import edge — it's a string that has to agree across a form field, a server action, and the schema. That's precisely the shape crimes exists to catch, and the reason `agent_risk` must not collapse into severity. Warn, with `centralise_domain_literal` as the suggested fix.\n\n### `duplicated_role_status_plan_check` — 2 warn\n`crime_00018` (`\"paid\"`), `crime_00019` (`\"pending\"`) · `severity: medium (0.55)` · `agent_risk: 0.36` · `confidence: 0.70`\n\nThe joint-highest severity in the report, and the evidence names the drift precondition explicitly: **2 distinct expression shapes** — `invoice.status === \"paid\"` in the form, bare `status === \"paid\"` in `status.tsx`. Two shapes over one rule is how the badge and the form stop agreeing.\n\nHeld at warn rather than block for two reasons: `confidence` is 0.70 (lowest of the medium-severity findings), and the \"4 files\" is 2 real files × the final/starter copy — inside one app it's 2 call sites in adjacent modules, which is under the threshold where a policy helper pays for itself.\n\n### `sync_io_in_hotpath` — 6 findings, **one** warn comment\n`severity: low (0.40)` · `agent_risk: 0.45 / 0.37` · `confidence: 0.90`\n\nHighest `agent_risk` in the entire report, and the charge is mis-scoped: all six are `fs.readdirSync`/`readFileSync` in `basics/*/lib/posts.js`, inside `getSortedPostsData` / `getAllPostIds` — the build-time data sources for `getStaticProps`/`getStaticPaths`. Blocking the event loop at build time is not a hot path, which is why severity correctly sits at `low` while the detector's `tier: domain` classification can't see the Next.js data-fetching contract.\n\nThe 0.45 is still earned, though, and it's a forward-looking risk rather than a present defect: an agent that copies this pattern into an App Router Server Component moves it onto a request path. Worth one consolidated comment saying that — not six identical ones, since the six are the same file across six tutorial steps.\n\n### `oversized_raster` — 6 warn\n`severity: low (0.40)` · `agent_risk: 0.04` · `confidence: 0.95`\n\n`hero-desktop.png` at 486.6 KB (×2 copies), `opengraph-image.png` at 237.5 KB (×2), `large-image.jpg` at 422.4 KB (×2). This is the one place I'm deliberately warning **against** the agent-risk signal: 0.04 is correct — no agent will reason wrongly about a PNG — but ~1.4 MB of unoptimised raster on a dashboard's first paint is a user-facing cost with a mechanical fix (`shrink_raster`, effort `quick`, `confidence` 0.95). Warn on the performance axis, explicitly not on the agent-risk axis.\n\n### `direct_date` — 1 of 3 warn\n`crime_00069` · `app/lib/actions.ts:57` · `severity: low (0.35)` · `agent_risk: 0.28`\n\nJust below my warn threshold, promoted by one fact the evidence supplies: the reading is in the server action, so the value is **persisted**, not rendered. The detector's own note (\"none decide a branch — all record or render\") is what keeps it at severity 0.35, and it's right that nothing branches on it — but an unfixed clock on a written record is untestable in a way a formatted display isn't. Low-priority warn; `inject_clock`, effort `small`.\n\n---\n\n## Ignore (79)\n\n### `exact_duplicate_block` — 46 findings, all ignore\n`severity: medium (0.55)` · `agent_risk: 0.28 – 0.31` · `confidence: 0.95`\n\n**46% of the report, zero of it actionable.** Every one of these 46 pairs a file with its own sibling copy — `final-example` ↔ `starter-example`, `api-routes-starter` ↔ `basics-final` ↔ `demo`, `seo` ↔ `seo/demo`. Not a single finding is a within-app duplicate. Deduplicating them would mean deleting the tutorial.\n\nThe count is further inflated by nesting: `crime_00042` (`seed/route.ts:7-30`) contains `crime_00043` (`:19-26`); `crime_00049` (`customers/table.tsx:6-120`) contains both `crime_00050` (`:22-60`) and `crime_00051` (`:84-111`). The same duplication is being counted at multiple granularities, so 46 overstates even the raw phenomenon.\n\nThe right response is a **config change, not a code change** — exclude the sibling example directories, or scope-class them as fixtures. High `confidence` (0.95) here is honest: crimes is certain the bytes match. It just can't know the match is the intent.\n\n### `raster_should_be_vector` — 12 ignore\n`severity: low (0.35)` · `agent_risk: 0.05`\n\nAll twelve are `public/customers/*.png` at 56×56 or 60×60 — **avatar photographs of people**, six of them duplicated across the example pair. A dimension threshold can't distinguish a headshot from an icon, and \"re-export the vector source\" has no meaning for a photo. Clean detector false positive; worth reporting upstream as detector feedback rather than to the PR author.\n\n### `design_token_escape` — 8 ignore\n`severity: low (0.40)` · `agent_risk: 0.07` · `confidence: 0.75`\n\nRepeated `px 18px` / `px 9px` / `px 38px` across the forms, login form, and skeletons — i.e. icon sizing and skeleton dimensions, four of the eight files being copies of the other four. `agent_risk` 0.07 is crimes telling you it doesn't believe an agent gets this wrong, and at `confidence` 0.75 it isn't certain these are even design-system escapes. Nothing to act on pre-merge.\n\n### `commented_out_code` — 6 ignore\n`crime_00011`–`00015`, `00017` · `severity: low (0.25)` · `confidence: 0.80 – 0.86`\n\nThe 2–8 line remainder. `crime_00013`/`00014` are the giveaway — 2 lines whose first code-like line is `console.log('Fetching revenue data...')`, adjacent to an `await`. That is the Next.js tutorial's own \"uncomment this to see the artificial delay\" instruction, not a corpse. Same for `crime_00011` (`async function listInvoices()`, 7 lines). Severity 0.25 and the sagging confidence (0.80–0.86 vs 0.90 for the big blocks) both point the same way.\n\n### `direct_date` — 2 ignore\n`crime_00028`, `crime_00029` · `utils.ts:14` in both copies\n\n`new Date()` inside `formatDateToLocal`. The reading is rendered, never branched on, and it's the same line in two copies of one file. Injecting a clock into a date formatter is ceremony.\n\n### `singular_plural_type_mismatch` — 2 ignore\n`severity: low (0.30)` · `agent_risk: 0.28` · `confidence: 0.70`\n\n`revenue: Revenue[]`. `revenue` is a mass noun — \"revenues\" would be worse English and worse code. The finding self-flags as a `v1 detector`, and the suggested action already names the escape hatch: add `revenue` to `detectors.options.singular_plural_type_mismatch.allowedNames`. Config, not review.\n\n### `deep_import` — 2 ignore\n`severity: low (0.35)` · `agent_risk: 0.07`\n\n`react-syntax-highlighter/dist/cjs/styles/prism` — that *is* the documented path for that package's themes; there's no public-surface alternative to migrate to. The `use_public_entry` action has nowhere to go. Suppress in config. (Both instances are the same file in `seo` and `seo/demo`.)\n\n### `duplicate_component_shape` — 1 ignore\n`crime_00068` · `severity: medium (0.55)` · `agent_risk: 0.28` · `confidence: 0.70`\n\nA `<div>` subtree shared between `create-form.tsx` and `edit-form.tsx` — 4 occurrences that are 2 forms × 2 copies. A create/edit pair sharing a field-wrapper shape is the expected shape of a React form pair, and at `confidence` 0.70 the premature extraction is likelier to cost more than the duplication does.\n\n---\n\n## The finding the petty detectors are all shadows of\n\nEvery cluster above resolves to the same root: **this PR ships two complete copies of the dashboard app plus six copies of the basics tutorial.** The 46 duplicate blocks, the `\"status\"` scatter across 5 files, `\"paid\"`/`\"pending\"` across 4, the doubled hero images and design-token escapes — all of it is one structural fact refracted through eleven detectors.\n\nThat's outside the \"petty / single-file\" scope this review was scoped to, and it's a deliberate property of a tutorial repo, so it isn't a merge block either. But it's the thing to fix first if this were a product: `crimes` will keep reporting ~50 medium findings per scan until the sibling copies are excluded in `crimes.config.json`, and until then the 20 warns above are buried under noise they didn't earn.",
